  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n- Dense Fog Saturday Morning; Patchy Fog on Sunday\n\n High pressure will continue dominate the weather pattern with \nlight winds and warmer air through the weekend. The light winds, \nhigh pressure, clearing skies is a great recipe for morning fog. \nThere is some question if it forms later. However, the flow from \nthe east should allow for moisture to converge overnight and fog \nto form across the region. Models remain consistent with highs 10 \nto 15 degrees above normal with Max temps in the upper 60s to low \n70s across the lower Peninsula. \n\nThe flow will shift Saturday night into Sunday and this will \nlessen the chance for fog development. However, given radiational\ncooling, clearing skies and light winds overall, patchy fog will \nbe again possible Sunday morning, though it should be less dense \nthan Friday and Saturday. \n\n- Pattern Change ahead with chances for rain beyond midweek\n\nThe upper level pattern has the high shifting southward and\nflattening next week. However, sfc high pressure should remain\nstout enough to keep any moisture bands to only effect the\nnorthern half of the lower Peninsula Monday and Tuesday. The \nensembles diverge on timing but the cold front associated with a \nlow pressure system tracking from the Canadian Rockies to Hudson \nBay will likely bring cooler air into Michigan dropping highs \ncloser to normal, in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\n Warm air advection midweek will correspond with a deepening\ntrough that will swing through the region and is the next chance \nshowers. Pwats are potent so about a quarter inch of rainfall is\npossible. \n\nModels continue to diverge after in the movement, timing and\npostilion of the upper level pattern. So a continued low \nconfidence in the forecast for the end of next week.",
